 "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, a mostly Lean R ratings consensus, and market pricing near 70 percent for Flint keep the GOP favored in this open seat, but September polling shows a genuine dead heat and outside money has not broken the race open, leaving only a modest edge.", "watch": "If the Democratic-favorable national environment starts showing up in district polling, or Libertarian Nick Sheehy draws disproportionately from Republican voters, the dead heat could tip to Forstag.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Ryan Zinke (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Montana's western congressional seat is open for the first time since the district was drawn, with Republican Ryan Zinke retiring and both parties contesting the replacement. The district leans Republican by about five points, and most race raters call the contest Lean Republican, though a couple rate it a Tossup. Prediction markets give the Republican roughly a 70 percent chance.\n\nThe polling record is thin: a September survey from a Democratic-affiliated pollster put Aaron Flint and Sam Forstag within two points, and a university poll a few days earlier had Flint up one. Fundraising is nearly even between the two, but outside groups have spent heavily on Flint's behalf while Forstag has drawn far less support. A Libertarian is on the ballot and could complicate the arithmetic in a close finish.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.3744, "margin": -1.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3797, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3466, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3473, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3145, "margin": -2.5}]}